ne gaddy packed sustained winds of  168 kilometers an hour  threat to national security ahead  allegations of funds being funneled into  canada  from iran  canada's spy agency csis is  investigating alleged attempts by iran  to secretly wire millions of dollars  into this country in violation of  international sanctions  global news has obtained documents that  point to money being funneled from iran  to a toronto currency exchange  owned by an iranian expat as mike delay  reports the money may have been used to  conduct  foreign influence behind the massive  doors of his north toronto mansion  iranian expat ali reza ongaei lives a  life of privilege  but since arriving in canada in 2008 his  attempts at gaining canadian citizenship  have been put on hold  as the canadian security intelligence  service looks into his business dealings  documents obtained by global news show  csis is investigating ongai for  assisting the government of iran in the  clandestine wiring of monies into canada  the csis report alleges the money came  from bank saterat  which the u.s treasury alleges iran uses  to fund hamas and hezbollah  ceases assesses the funds to be in the  millions  but for what purpose if the information  in  the report is true it is unique in the  sense that  often when we talk about threat related  activities with financing  it is outside of canada so individuals  sending money outside of canada  often to support violent extremist  groups but in this case with clandestine  foreign influence it may very well be  activity inside of canada  the united nations began imposing  sanctions on iran in 2006  in response to its nuclear program  canada imposed additional sanctions in  2010  the same year ongai opened ong currency  exchange in toronto  to get around the sanctions ongei  admitted to css that money was  transferred first from iran  to the uae before making its way to  canada  and into ong's accounts cesares wrote  that  ongai knows circumventing economic  sanctions is clearly illegal  and that he admitted he'd been doing it  for three years  ongae told global news he doesn't  support the iranian regime  and that the money was transferred for  clients toronto lawyer and human rights  activist kaveh sharooz  says there's been rumors in the iranian  community about vast sums of money  arriving in canada for the purpose  of influencing canadian foreign policy  and unfortunately what they're finding  is that  you know the iranian regime extends its  tentacles into canada  after ong's registration was revoked in  2012  ongai became sole director of a company  that sells surgical equipment  his finances remain a mystery his first  wife told the court in divorce  proceedings he was worth  25 million dollars while he claimed to  have earned as little as 7 500 in annual  income  and while his latest appeal for  citizenship was dismissed on november  9th  he told global news he plans to continue  fighting  microlight global news toronto  there is a lot more detail on this  complex story you can read stuart bell  and sam cooper's investigation on our  website globalnews.ca  global national christmas plights next  canadians abroad consider their options  for the holidays  it's hard to tell loved ones i won't be  home for christmas but millions of  canadians are not making travel plans  for the holidays  as tired as we are of being told to find  new ways to celebrate  this is going to be a quarantine kind of  christmas  if you can gather at all it will be in  small groups  and for canadians living overseas it's  just not worth the trip  or the risk redmond shannon spoke to  some of them  the one christmas wish for many  canadians who live in britain  is simply to get back home i've been in  tears several times  over the past month or two about not  being able to go  um my parents are really upset um my  whole family is and we've been trying to  figure out ways  to make this work and it's just it's not  possible  and we understand it and